5	 * @test random_access_sweep
6	 * @parblock
7	 * Random-access address-space sweep: each worker thread maps a large
8	 * anonymous region and, every iteration, picks a RANDOM offset and a
9	 * RANDOM block length (log-uniform from 64 B to 2 MiB, covering the
10	 * L1/L2/L3/DRAM scales), fills the block with random data, runs a NEON
11	 * read-modify-write transform over it, and verifies the result
12	 * byte-exactly against a same-iteration scalar golden.
13	 *
14	 * Rationale (SDC randomization hardening H5'): the suite's address
15	 * coverage was bimodal — either large-and-fixed (memcpy_l3's 8 MiB
16	 * fallback, mmu_stress_arm's 16 MiB walk) or small-and-random (mfence's
17	 * 32 KiB random index). NO test combined a large mmap with random
18	 * offsets AND varying block sizes, which is exactly the shape that
19	 * sweeps cache sets/ways, TLB entries and DRAM banks unpredictably —
20	 * the address dimension the CPU-122 hunt needs mapped.
21	 *
22	 * Knobs (-O random_access_sweep.<key>=<value>):
23	 *   size_mb=N    per-thread region size in MiB (default: memory-adaptive,
24	 *                min(512 MiB, MemAvailable*0.6/threads), floor 8 MiB)
25	 *   mode=SEQ     sequential block walk (address control; default RAND)
26	 *   valmode=PATTERN  0xAA/0x55 alternating fill instead of random
27	 *   valmode=SPECIALS high-Hamming 8-word table (like lsu_store_forward)
28	 * @endparblock
29	 */
